
====================================================================================================
S1. The universal rate, re-derived: omega * Im K = a0/2c EXACTLY, for every omega
====================================================================================================
  On the oscillatory branch K(-w^2+i0) = exp[i*arcsin(1/2w)], so Im K = sin phi = 1/(2w).
  A mode of frequency omega therefore decays/grows at  omega * Im K:
      rate = omega * 1/(2w)  with w = omega c/a0   ->   a0/(2*c)
  [OK] the rate is INDEPENDENT of omega (sympy: d/dw = 0)
  [OK] the rate equals a0/2c exactly
  This is why the scale is universal: the kernel's Im part falls as 1/omega and exactly
  cancels the omega in front. Every oscillatory mode in the universe shares one rate.

====================================================================================================
S2. THE LOAD-BEARING SPLIT -- orbit real, perturbations complex. Interrogated, not assumed.
====================================================================================================
  KERNEL_THEORY.md asserts: under the first-moment closure the ORBIT's secular drift is
  exactly zero because K(a^2/a0^2) is real, while the phase still acts on PERTURBATIONS.
  That split is what the whole door rests on, so state the three possible readings:

  READING 1 -- 'literal frequency-domain closure for everything'. The orbit is itself an
    oscillation at Omega, so it samples the cut too and drifts at a0/2c. KERNEL_THEORY.md
    Finding C already declares this DEAD, twice over: it fails the RAR outright, AND it
    predicts ~0.4 m/yr in the Earth-Sun distance against a ~cm/yr ephemeris bound.
  READING 2 -- 'first moment for everything'. The operator is replaced by its u-contraction
    moment |a|^2 for ALL motion, perturbations included. Then the response is real and
    instantaneous everywhere, there is no phase anywhere, and the drift is exactly zero for
    perturbations too. THE DOOR SHUTS -- no observable, sign stays postulated.
  READING 3 -- 'first moment for the DC amplitude, frequency domain for the AC response'.
    The orbit's zero-frequency/secular part is the first moment (real, no drift); a
    perturbation at kappa is a genuinely nonzero-frequency probe and samples the cut. This
    is the reading KERNEL_THEORY.md's sentence encodes, and the ONLY one with a live door.

  HONEST STATUS OF READING 3. It is not derived anywhere in the corpus. It is the natural
  linear-response split (DC susceptibility real, AC susceptibility complex, which is what
  Kramers-Kronig gives for any causal kernel), and it is consistent -- but a DERIVATION
  would need the closure applied to a two-timescale expansion, which does not exist yet.
  So everything below is CONDITIONAL ON READING 3, and that condition is the door's real
  cost. Under Reading 2 the door shuts immediately and the sign stays postulated.
  [OK] the split is stated as a conditional assumption, not smuggled in as a result

====================================================================================================
S3. The universal rate in usable units, both footings
====================================================================================================
  canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   a0/2c = 1.5611e-19 1/s   tau =   203.0 Gyr   rate = 4.9263e-12 /yr
  alt rho_total cH0              a0/2c = 1.8846e-19 1/s   tau =   168.1 Gyr   rate = 5.9473e-12 /yr
  Footing spread on the rate: 1.207x -- unusually TIGHT for this corpus, because the
  rate is linear in a0 with no other footing-sensitive input.
  [OK] the two footings differ by only 1.21x, so a confrontation is not footing-hostage

====================================================================================================
S4. ORBITAL-ENERGY channel -- validate Peters, then price what Reading 1 already costs
====================================================================================================
  Validation gate: reproduce the MEASURED Pbdot from masses+Pb+e alone. If this fails, no
  number below is trustworthy and the script exits nonzero.
  pulsar         Pbdot Peters      Pbdot obs    ratio
  B1913+16       -2.40360e-12   -2.42300e-12   0.9920
  [OK] B1913+16: Peters reproduces the measured Pbdot to 0.8% -- implementation validated
  J0737-3039     -1.24834e-12   -1.24792e-12   1.0003
  [OK] J0737-3039: Peters reproduces the measured Pbdot to 0.0% -- implementation validated

  Now the orbital channel. GR is confirmed at these fractional precisions, so any EXTRA
  universal energy drift must hide inside the residual. Reported in SIGMA, not in vague
  'room', so the strength of the statement is visible:
  pulsar        GR conf.  |Pbdot/Pb| GR  1-sigma (1/yr)  a0/2c (1/yr)  -> sigma
  B1913+16       1.6e-03     2.7180e-09      4.3488e-12    4.9263e-12      1.1s
  J0737-3039     1.3e-04     4.4591e-09      5.7968e-13    4.9263e-12      8.5s
  So the double pulsar excludes a universal a0/2c ENERGY drift at 8.5 sigma,
  while B1913+16 alone reaches only 1.1 sigma -- the constraint is carried by
  J0737-3039's 1.3e-4 Pbdot agreement, not by the Hulse-Taylor system.
  [OK] the orbital channel excludes the rate at 8.5 sigma -- a SECOND, independent kill of Reading 1 / the literal closure, from pulsars rather than Solar-System ephemerides

====================================================================================================
S5. PERTURBATION channel (the live one under Reading 3) -- real observability, not a ratio
====================================================================================================
  CORRECTING MY OWN FIRST FRAMING. I first priced this as 'what fraction of GR's own edot
  is the effect', which came out ~1e-3 and made the test look nearly in reach. That framing
  is wrong: it silently assumes edot_GR is itself measured, and in these systems it is NOT
  detected at all. The honest question is what the DATA can resolve.

  The observable is a universal fractional drift edot/e = -/+ a0/2c. Over a timing baseline
  T that produces a fractional eccentricity change (a0/2c)*T, to be compared against how
  well e itself is known. Using published e precisions and a 20-yr baseline:
  pulsar         sigma_e/e   resolvable rate  a0/2c (1/yr)   shortfall
  B1913+16        1.62e-06        8.1020e-08    4.9263e-12    1.6e+04x
  J0737-3039      1.03e-05        5.1266e-07    4.9263e-12    1.0e+05x
  Also for reference, GR's own edot in these systems, which is NOT detected either:
    B1913+16     edot_GR/e = 9.238e-10/yr   (a0/2c is 5.3e-03 of it)
    J0737-3039   edot_GR/e = 4.577e-09/yr   (a0/2c is 1.1e-03 of it)
  [OK] binary-pulsar eccentricity is short of the rate by ~2e+04x -- the pulsar perturbation channel does NOT reach it, and not marginally

  THE ONE SUB-CHANNEL THAT IS NOT OBVIOUSLY SHUT: planetary ephemerides. Same universal
  fractional rate, but a 2-century baseline and metre-level orbit knowledge. Required
  sensitivity on an ANOMALOUS eccentricity drift, in the units the ephemeris literature uses:
    Mercury  e = 0.205630  ->  need |de/dt|_anom < 4.93e-10 /century fractional  =  1.01e-10 in e per century
    Mars     e = 0.093412  ->  need |de/dt|_anom < 4.93e-10 /century fractional  =  4.60e-11 in e per century
  I am NOT asserting the published bound here, because I have not verified one in this
  session, and inventing it would be worse than leaving the door ajar. What IS established
  is the target number. Checking it against INPOP/EPM anomalous-secular bounds is a cheap,
  bounded literature task, and it is the single thing that would decide this channel.
  [OK] the ephemeris sub-channel is left explicitly OPEN with its target quantified, rather than closed on an unverified number

====================================================================================================
S6. LONG-BASELINE lever -- a fractional rate is best attacked over ~10 Gyr
====================================================================================================
  A rate of 1/(200 Gyr) is hopeless on a 20-yr baseline but not on a cosmological one.
  Cumulative fractional change in a perturbation amplitude over cosmic time:
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   over   1.0 Gyr:  +0.494% (gain branch) /  -0.491% (damping branch)
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   over  10.0 Gyr:  +5.050% (gain branch) /  -4.807% (damping branch)
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   over  13.8 Gyr:  +7.035% (gain branch) /  -6.572% (damping branch)
  So over a Hubble time the effect is only ~6.6% in amplitude. The two sign
  branches are separated by about twice that:
    gain/damping amplitude ratio after 13.8 Gyr = 14.6%
  [OK] the two sign branches differ by only 15% after a full Hubble time -- so even a perfect 10-Gyr-baseline population statistic needs better than ~5% control of ordinary heating/damping to separate them
  Candidate 10-Gyr baselines and why each is hard:
   * disk age-velocity-dispersion relation: ~7% signal against GMC/spiral/bar/merger heating
     that is uncertain at the tens-of-per-cent level. Systematics-dominated.
   * wide-binary eccentricity distribution over 10 Gyr: same ~7%, against an initial
     distribution that is itself inferred. Also it is the SAME sample as the gate-fork
     front, so a shared systematic would couple two supposedly independent tests.
   * globular-cluster binary eccentricities: 10-Gyr baseline, but dynamical encounters
     dominate the eccentricity evolution outright.
  NOTE the asymmetry that makes this NOT a stability argument: a 7% growth over a Hubble
  time is not catastrophic, so the GAIN branch is NOT excluded by 'the universe would have
  shaken itself apart'. That was worth checking and it does not work.

====================================================================================================
S7. The gravitational-wave route -- closed by the corpus's OWN v11 theorem
====================================================================================================
  A universal amplitude drift on a propagating wave would be a modified GW luminosity
  distance: h ~ exp(-rate * D/c), i.e. a percent-level effect at cosmological distance:
    GW170817               D =     40 Mpc  ->  amplitude change  0.064%
    1 Gpc dark siren       D =   1000 Mpc  ->  amplitude change  1.594%
    5 Gpc, ET/LISA era     D =   5000 Mpc  ->  amplitude change  7.720%
  That would be a genuinely fresh front -- the corpus has NO GW lane. But it is closed
  from the inside: the v11 work (Zenodo 21284144) established that the TT x frame vertex
  is EXACTLY ZERO and graviton-frame mixing is closed (R.uu = -H^2 P_perp exact, but the
  commutators are algebraic; k0^2 -> H^2, never k_perp^2). The frame field does not couple
  to transverse-traceless gravitons at the vertex, so this kernel does not damp GWs.
  Recording it as CLOSED rather than quietly leaving it as a hope.
  [OK] GW lane closed by v11's TT x frame vertex theorem, not left as an open hope

====================================================================================================
VERDICT -- is the sign closable by this channel?
====================================================================================================
  NO, not with data now on the books. Quantified:
   * The rate is universal and sharp: a0/2c = 4.93e-12/yr, tau = 203 Gyr,
     with only a 1.21x footing spread. That part of the door is real and is the
     cleanest system-independent number the framework owns.
   * ORBITAL channel: EXCLUDES the rate at 8.5 sigma (J0737-3039; B1913+16 alone
     reaches only 1.1 sigma). But that channel is Reading 1, already dead on RAR
     grounds, so this is a CONFIRMATION of a known kill from a new direction (pulsars, not
     Solar-System ephemerides) -- not a new constraint on the live theory.
   * PERTURBATION channel (the live one): binary-pulsar eccentricity is short by
     ~2e+04x. Not marginal. My first pass priced this as a ratio to GR's own edot
     (~1e-3) which made it look nearly in reach; that framing was wrong, because edot_GR is
     itself undetected in these systems. Corrected above.
   * LONG-BASELINE channel: signal is ~7% over a Hubble time and the two sign
     branches differ by ~15%, against 10-Gyr heating systematics that are worse
     than that. Not reachable either, and the wide-binary version would share a sample
     with the gate-fork front.
   * GW channel: closed by the framework's own v11 vertex theorem.

  SO THE DOOR SHUTS -- but it shuts on SENSITIVITY, not on principle, and it leaves three
  things behind that are worth more than a null usually is:
   1. The sign postulate is now KNOWN to be unclosable by the dissipative channel at
      current precision, with the required precision quantified rather than guessed. That
      retires a route instead of leaving it as a vague hope, which is what 'forward = data'
      had been resting on.
   2. A SECOND, INDEPENDENT KILL of the literal frequency-domain closure, from binary
      pulsars (8.5 sigma, carried by J0737-3039) rather than from Solar-System
      ephemerides. Finding C's verdict no longer rests on one system.
   3. READING 3 IS NOW EXPOSED AS AN UNDERIVED LOAD-BEARING ASSUMPTION. The corpus states
      'the phase acts only on perturbations' as though settled; it is not. Under Reading 2
      there is no perturbation drift at all. Deriving the DC/AC split from a two-timescale
      treatment of the closure is a real, bounded, unstarted theory task -- and it also
      controls the sigma-spread cross term repriced on 2026-07-30, so one calculation
      settles two things.

  WHAT WOULD REOPEN IT: any system with a >=1e-6 fractional measurement of an oscillation
  amplitude over a >=decade baseline, where ordinary damping is modelled to that level.
  Pulsar timing arrays and next-generation ephemerides are the places to look; neither is
  there yet for a PERTURBATION amplitude as opposed to an orbital period.
====================================================================================================
